#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b673724a-4915-517c-b9a0-fe79b8e7faa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289305} {:answer/id #uuid "aec1f854-7d74-50f2-a59b-e2ff15f06aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289306} {:answer/id #uuid "7b50d5f9-72ad-547c-a124-76efa4e14d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289307} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bf9be5-b982-5166-9542-007bcdd73298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289308} {:answer/id #uuid "24847843-649b-595c-82b8-e8e87f70614a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1bf9be5-b982-5166-9542-007bcdd73298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289308} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42fdd2c-3928-5581-8f96-17bc27665aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cfe0cab-3f64-5782-9a2c-dd556be2b484" :question/math-academy-id "q-162202" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca92b4c-bc03-577b-8a42-11e52981c0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289312} {:answer/id #uuid "0e950484-dbc4-522d-81c1-37497abf293f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289313} {:answer/id #uuid "fff3f9d7-1b1b-5880-b370-424998a56ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289314} {:answer/id #uuid "62aaa7c9-3ff7-51da-b2b9-a1e2d05ccad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} {:answer/id #uuid "549ca8fa-9331-5ead-a01a-20addbd7da9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62aaa7c9-3ff7-51da-b2b9-a1e2d05ccad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ad26f2-6912-513b-b733-e76e602210c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bea7908-25f3-50cf-9a41-019383192d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-162208" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0231cc5aaf661cc873f18ff676a03d66927be48b49d38649efc45860197d18c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."}]}
